[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Every summer your building dumps a mountain of heat into the air just to stay cool. And every winter it burns fuel to make all that heat right back. Now, what if you could catch the heat that you\'re throwing away in July, park it underground until January, and then do the same thing with the winter\'s cold'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to get you through next August? That\'s a real thing that you can build. It\'s a kind of thermal battery buried under our feet, and it\'s been quietly [music] heating and cooling buildings in the Netherlands for more than 30 years. The wild part?'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; The US Department of Energy proved that it could work here decades ago. And then we just basically forgot about it. Take the campus at Eindhoven University, home of one of the most impressive geothermal projects of its kind anywhere. The Dutch call it warmte-koudeopslag.'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, this system is not some indecipherable secret like the Dutch language. &gt;&gt; [laughter] &gt;&gt; And now I just butchered it. [music] In the rest of the world it\'s called aquifer thermal energy storage, or ATES. So, let\'s dig in to how you turn the'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ground beneath your feet [music] into a battery for heat, why a country better known for tulips and canals is running away with it, and whether it could ever catch on here in America. I\'m Matt Ferrell. Welcome to Undecided. This video is brought to you by'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Microcenter. [music] When my team and I dug into this, I was expecting a typical story about an advanced tech that\'s [music] so expensive that it can\'t earn its money back, or that it only works under conditions so specific that they barely exist anywhere. What I found instead was'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'something more complex, but potentially much more manageable. So, let\'s set the table by getting into the uh water table. To explain ATES, let\'s start with the A or aquifers. These are porous'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'underground formations of sand, gravel, and rock that hold water. Despite the name, an aquifer isn\'t a big underground cave or lake. Picture a giant sponge of sand and gravel with water filling the gaps. And I don\'t want to alarm you, but'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you may be standing over one right now. But, don\'t worry. They\'re stable, huge, and crucially for ATES, they stay close to the average surface temperature. The Earth, it turns out, is a fantastic insulator. That\'s exactly why the sponge'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can work like a battery. If you pumped hot water into an open cavern, the heat would quickly bleed away. But surrounded by all that sand and rock, it stays put. It helps that water has an unusually high heat capacity, too. [music]'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, it holds onto a lot of that heat without escaping. Now, we need to address the T, the E, and the S, which is the thermal energy storage. In the summer, when the ATES system is in cooling mode, groundwater is pumped up through a heat exchanger,'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'where it takes heat and shoves it back underground into one side of the aquifer that stores warm water. In winter, that warm water is pumped back up for heating through a heat exchanger, and then it heads back down to the cooler water side'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of the aquifer. Now, that Dutch animation will make a lot more sense. ATES works in a similar way to air source heat pumps, which use outdoor air to warm and cool buildings. But when it\'s an extremely hot or cold'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'outside, air source heat pumps lose efficiency. ATES is powerful at these exact moments, as it\'s able to take stored thermal energy from an entirely different season. When it\'s winter outside, it can still be summer down in'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the aquifer. ATES is great for small apartment blocks, hospitals, and clusters of urban buildings. At Eindhoven, the system covers 19 different buildings. And the benefits of this are potentially huge, because the'

[03:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'energy used by heating and cooling buildings is huge. About a quarter of primary energy goes to heating [music] and cooling buildings. So, how good is it? Well, the number that jumps out at me is cooling. A well-designed ATES'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'system [music] can be roughly five times more efficient at cooling than a conventional geothermal system. And the reason is a trick called free cooling. Now, [music] most systems, including a normal geothermal heat pump like the one'

[03:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'ve got, run a compressor every time they cool. ATES mostly doesn\'t. It spends all winter banking genuinely cold water underground. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Then in summer, it just pumps that stored chill through a heat exchanger'

[03:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for a little more than the cost of running a pump. Against an ordinary air source heat pump, the gap is even wider, up to 10 times more efficient for cooling. This one got me. I have a ground source heat pump loop in my own'

[03:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'house, so I figured that was about as good as it gets. It turns out mine still runs a compressor every time it cools, while [music] ATES just coasts on the cold that it banked all winter. Fair caveat, mine is a closed loop for one house.'

[04:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; An ATES system is an open aquifer fed system for whole districts, so it\'s not just a swap I could have made at home. Still, it\'s a little humbling. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; And cooling is only part of it. Heating runs up to four times more efficient than a gas furnace, and the emission'

[04:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'savings can reach 75%. And the best of all is cheap enough to run that the whole system usually pays for itself in two to 10 years, of course faster where energy is more expensive. I know I\'m making ATES sound perfect and'

[04:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'flawless, like it\'s the Dutch language or something, but of course there are drawbacks that we need to mention. And we\'ll get into the drawbacks in a second, but speaking of something that saves you money, let\'s talk about today\'s sponsor, Micro'

[04:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Center. And quick confession, I\'m an Apple guy, but I\'ve built my own PCs for decades and I still can\'t quit it. My latest is a tiny gaming rig for the living room, hooked to the big screen for my Steam library. My [music] Mac Studio is the brain of my smart home, it'

[04:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'runs Home Assistant, handles my automations, and powers [music] my own self-hosted AI voice assistant. All local, all mine. None of it leaves the house. [music] For years, whenever I needed a part fast and affordable, Micro Center was my move.'

[05:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I practically lived at the Cambridge store, so I\'m happy they\'re sponsoring this one. Their back-to-school deals event is on now, and the deals are genuinely good. The link\'s in the description if you want to browse. And two store updates, too. Austin, Texas, you\'re finally getting a Micro Center.'

[05:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Sign up with the link below for [music] a free 128 gig flash drive and grab it when the doors open later this year. And Columbus, Ohio, the grand reopening of your store is happening on August 22nd. Sign up for your own free 128 gig flash'

[05:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'drive for the grand reopening later this year. And don\'t worry, it stays open the whole time. And for everything else tech, check out Micro Center news. All the links are in the description. Thanks to Micro Center and to all of you for supporting the channel.'

[05:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Okay, back underground because for all that promise, ATES does have a few real drawbacks. The aquifer wells for ATES systems can corrode, decreasing their efficiency. As you might imagine, you\'d need to be very careful with'

[06:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'groundwater. Mud and silt can get in the way over time and even worse, ATES systems can stimulate [music] bacterial growth by injecting warm water back into the aquifer. And these multiplying bacteria can make a slimy biofilm that can obstruct pores in the ground. Now,'

[06:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m not a hydrology expert, but slimy biofilms are the kind of thing you do not want to be making more of underground. But these problems are manageable and even the Department of Energy declared ATES ready for commercialization more'

[06:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'than 7 years ago. So, if ATES is the greatest, where is it? Because it certainly hasn\'t taken off here in America. To understand why that is or maybe more accurately why not, we need to see if there are places that has been'

[06:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'successful and what might be learned from those places. Which we\'ll get to in a bit. Before any of that, let\'s just see which places this tech might be feasible at all. To make ATES [music] work, you need two'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'things: an aquifer and an above-ground temperature that gets hot in the summer and cold in the winter. If the average temperature of a place is super low or super high, you\'re not going to get the benefits. This is bad news for viewers in Anchorage or Death Valley. But while'

[07:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'s true that not every place has the right geological and climate conditions, there\'s no shortage of places that do. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; A 2019 study found that 27% of all land is in a good or very good zone for ATES. Okay, but are those zones in places that'

[07:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'people actually live? Also, yes. Broad swaths of North America, Asia, and Western Europe are perfect for ATES. And moreover, land has been identified in the urban zones which have most of the buildings and complexes that can make'

[07:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use of this technology. A potential market study commissioned by the New York State Energy Research and Development Authority found around 1,800 potential buildings that could benefit from ATES in Queens and the Brooklyn boroughs of New York City. A separate'

[07:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'study found that ATES for heating and cooling could reduce consumption of natural gas and electricity in the US by 40%. So, if this technology works and there\'s land to do it in America cuz we\'ve got a lot of it, why don\'t we have'

[08:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it? And could we? Do we just lack models to copy? Is this the case where the first mover just hasn\'t moved or what? Well, there\'s a land where the stroopwafels are fresh and the aquifers'

[08:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are porous. It\'s a country that built over 3,000 ATES projects in the last 30-something years. We\'re talking about the Netherlands, the heart of the global ATES know-how that contains something like 85% of all'

[08:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'global projects. But why the Netherlands? It\'s a combination of price and policy. Energy is much more expensive in the Netherlands than the US, which makes the case for efficiency gains more appealing. But on top of that, the Dutch'

[08:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'government has put in place serious regulations to reduce greenhouse gas emissions. It also probably helps that the water table is just 4 m underground across 90% of the country. The Dutch created an energy performance standard'

[08:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'requiring building owners to make their buildings efficient. Performance guidelines become stricter starting all the way back in the early \'90s. By 2022, the standard required buildings to be energy neutral or even energy positive.'

[09:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This requirement for building efficiency ratcheted up over decades and it spiked demand for ATES systems. But that demand didn\'t immediately make the Dutch masters of the aquifer battery. There were speed bumps or their Dutch'

[09:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'equivalent varkensstronkels along the way and I know I just butchered that word. &gt;&gt; [laughter] &gt;&gt; In the 1990s and the early 2000s, anyone who applied for a permit to build an ATES system was let into the market. The result? A bunch of sloppy, poorly'

[09:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'performing systems. In response, the Dutch created quality standards for every part of ATES from their design through their installation and operation. In order to make sure the wells would work for a long time, they'

[09:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'even made installing contractors responsible for their maintenance. This went well beyond the tech. It made Dutch ATES cheaper and better than any that has ever existed. The country is much further along than any other and is not'

[09:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'even close. I\'m talking about Dutch ATES success as if it\'s the product of thoughtful fostering of technology, regulations, and markets over decades. And it is those things. But it\'s also worth mentioning that the Dutch are just'

[10:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really insanely good at water management. To some extent, it can\'t be surprising that the country that has been pioneering control of water for centuries would figure out how to make aquifers into batteries first. So, the Dutch have had a head start, and they'

[10:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'even earned it. It took more than one sample project. They built a market and a regulatory framework geared towards ATES excellence over the long term. Given all that we know, let\'s think about how ATES might or might not work'

[10:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the US. Let\'s be honest here. I\'m not sure the Dutch example is going to be so easy to repeat here in the United States. With the current national energy policy is to send every newborn home from the'

[10:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hospital with a dripping sack of light sweet crude, metaphorically speaking. And it\'s not like no one here is thinking about making ATES work. The case for it, after all, is pretty good. Last year, there was an ATS system planned for a large'

[10:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'development in Minneapolis near the site where the US tested the technology over 40 years ago. But further into the planning stages, it turns out that the ATS system didn\'t pencil out. So, they\'re going with a different approach'

[11:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for their heating and cooling. Right now, ATS is expensive here in the US, and people are unfamiliar with the technology. The Dutch built 32 wells to power the ATS system at Eindhoven Science University, and we can\'t even get two.'

[11:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, these are significant barriers. A study modeling how ATES could work in a Chicago neighborhood found that although ATES could reduce petroleum use by 40%, it would cost 15 to 20% more than existing energy storage technologies.'

[11:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'However, over time the price of ATES would drop. The same studies first author, ATD Pereira, says, "After just a few years of developing ATES, we could easily break even. That\'s why it\'s quite'

[11:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'important that we start to invest in this research and start building real-world prototype systems." There are reasons to believe that these investments might actually happen. First off, there are still significant government funds that are left for new geothermal left over from the Biden'

[12:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'administration. Skyrocketing demand for cooling and water from data centers also provides a serious near-term incentive to develop the technology. And many of the cities and metro areas where ATES could work, like New York, will continue'

[12:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to push greater efficiency standards. On top of this, as climate change [music] brings even greater swings in seasonal temperatures, the case for having a clean technology to manage the swings by storing thermal energy in the ground'

[12:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'only gets stronger. Internationally, demand for heating and cooling up in the developing world, making the economic case for getting good ATES stronger still. When it comes to these kinds of systems, we\'re in a bit of a geothermal'

[12:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'chicken and a geothermal egg situation. This technology can work in the states. We have 10 kinds of proof, but someone just needs to lead. One project won\'t do it, and it\'s going to take time. The Dutch started all the way back in the'

[12:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'early 1990s, around the same time that the Department of Energy report concluded seasonal storage promised to significantly reduce the need to generate primary energy in the United States. With aquifer seen as the most cost-effective approach, this technology'

[13:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'does work, and it could work well here, too. All that remains is for people to do the hard grinding work of building it out. But what do you think? Will ATES take off here in the US? What do you think is'

